1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,000
Το Plex ζήτησε 120€ για να συνεχίσετε να βλέπετε από τις συσκευές σας.
Το πλήρωσα εγώ το ποσό.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:10,000
Αν χρησιμοποιείτε συχνά και μπορείτε,
 οποιαδήποτε συμβολή θα εκτιμηθεί.

3
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:18,430
Hey, Sein!

4
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,520
That mage said they'd take our photo.

5
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:31,480
A photo? What's that?

6
00:00:46,290 --> 00:00:48,450
Don't worry about it. Come on.

7
00:00:54,190 --> 00:00:57,370
This is the start of our legend!

8
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:28,040
<i>Like a fairy tale</i>

9
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,460
<i>It's a sign the story has reached its end</i>

10
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:33,380
<i>Like a single passage taken from</i>

11
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:35,010
<i>A journey that was too long</i>

12
00:01:35,010 --> 00:01:37,340
<i>Of the evil that once cast a shadow</i>

13
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:39,680
<i>Across this land</i>

14
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:42,010
<i>And the memories of the short journey</i>

15
00:01:42,010 --> 00:01:44,470
<i>With the hero who defeated it</i>

16
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:56,780
<i>The story ends</i>

17
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:59,280
<i>And the hero goes to sleep</i>

18
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:02,120
<i>Leaving this land with</i>

19
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:03,780
<i>Days of peace and quiet</i>

20
00:02:03,780 --> 00:02:06,290
<i>The passage of time heartlessly</i>

21
00:02:06,290 --> 00:02:08,460
<i>Makes people forget</i>

22
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:11,250
<i>Even the tracks left behind by his life</i>

23
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:13,040
<i>Begin to rust</i>

24
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:14,880
<i>Just the same</i>

25
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:17,170
<i>Your words and wishes and courage</i>

26
00:02:17,170 --> 00:02:20,510
<i>Are still inside of me</i>

27
00:02:20,510 --> 00:02:22,390
<i>And they live on</i>

28
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:24,930
<i>We chose the same path</i>

29
00:02:24,930 --> 00:02:27,060
<i>That's all it was supposed to be</i>

30
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:28,600
<i>Why is it that at some point</i>

31
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:32,860
<i>I wanted to understand the reason for</i>

32
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:34,230
<i>The tears that fall down my cheeks</i>

33
00:02:34,230 --> 00:02:35,320
<i>Even now</i>

34
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:37,690
<i>If I retrace the journey we walked together</i>

35
00:02:37,690 --> 00:02:41,240
<i>Even though you're not there</i>

36
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:42,910
<i>I know I'll be able to find it</i>

37
00:03:03,930 --> 00:03:06,080
Is he really a strong warrior?

38
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:10,440
He's a dwarf who's protected the village
we're heading to for almost 400 years.

39
00:03:10,750 --> 00:03:14,370
What's the life expectancy of dwarves?

40
00:03:14,370 --> 00:03:16,440
About 300 years.

41
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:18,770
His name is Old Man Voll.

42
00:03:18,770 --> 00:03:20,690
He's a long-lived friend of mine.

43
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:24,260
I want to chat with him before he dies.

44
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:26,170
That's not funny.

45
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:33,570
But it's true that there aren't many people
who can talk about the past with you.

46
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:37,280
Perhaps a detour like this one
isn't so bad once in a while.

47
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:40,810
Maybe I'll stay for a decade or so.

48
00:03:40,810 --> 00:03:42,760
We're staying one week at most.

49
00:04:03,570 --> 00:04:05,250
That's the village?

50
00:04:11,090 --> 00:04:12,230
Old Man Voll.

51
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:15,450
It's been a while.

52
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:21,360
Well? Doesn't he seem cool,
like an old, seasoned warrior?

53
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:23,340
He's way beyond old.

54
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:28,910
Remind me. Who are you?

55
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:30,400
He's totally decrepit.

56
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:32,390
It's me, Frieren.

57
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:36,840
Ah, yes.

58
00:04:39,010 --> 00:04:41,410
You're still pretending to be senile?

59
00:04:44,030 --> 00:04:44,790
Huh?

60
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:52,990
Most deaths in battle
are due to carelessness.

61
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:57,120
It's the greatest weakness
of both demons and people.

62
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:00,130
Can't stand, can you?

63
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:05,010
Even the most experienced warriors
can easily suffer fatal injuries

64
00:05:05,010 --> 00:05:08,340
if they're not attempting
to defend themselves.

65
00:05:09,830 --> 00:05:14,050
If I'd drawn my sword then,
you'd be short one leg.

66
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:17,660
Your strategies are as underhanded as ever.

67
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:20,030
Frieren.

68
00:05:20,370 --> 00:05:24,380
You are welcome here. Make yourself at home.

69
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:28,010
Wait, Frieren.

70
00:05:29,150 --> 00:05:30,350
I can't walk.

71
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,660
Stark, train with him while you're here.

72
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:35,940
Okay.

73
00:05:36,530 --> 00:05:38,970
He's one heck of an old man.

74
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:42,580
You're welcome to use this firewood.

75
00:05:43,150 --> 00:05:47,280
I'd like to stay here for a decade.
Find us some work.

76
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:50,470
Ms. Frieren, we're only
staying up to one week.

77
00:05:51,390 --> 00:05:52,830
Okay, fine.

78
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:11,190
It's a small village.

79
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:15,540
No one else would take the job,
so I appreciate it.

80
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:17,510
It's not like we have anything else to do.

81
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:22,940
Anyway, I had no idea
Old Man Voll had friends.

82
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:26,220
I've never seen him converse
so happily before.

83
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:28,770
He's a senile old man.

84
00:06:28,770 --> 00:06:30,730
Normally he struggles
to hold a conversation.

85
00:06:31,790 --> 00:06:37,680
He's protected the village from monsters and
demons for years, but nobody knows why.

86
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:41,200
He's like our protective deity.

87
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:44,590
He's been alone this whole time.

88
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:26,530
A week flies by, doesn't it?

89
00:07:26,870 --> 00:07:28,280
How is Stark?

90
00:07:29,070 --> 00:07:31,770
Humans develop quickly.

91
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:34,370
He's improved a fair bit.

92
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:35,570
I see.

93
00:07:37,390 --> 00:07:40,220
I had fun, Old Man Voll.

94
00:07:41,730 --> 00:07:44,480
I'm really grateful to you now.

95
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:49,930
You're the reason I got to know
Himmel and the others.

96
00:07:50,980 --> 00:07:53,940
Old Man Voll, why do you
defend this village?

97
00:07:54,730 --> 00:07:56,930
It's ancient history now.

98
00:07:57,450 --> 00:08:00,150
It doesn't matter.

99
00:08:00,630 --> 00:08:03,640
People don't risk their lives
for things that don't matter.

100
00:08:07,110 --> 00:08:13,930
I'm just defending the village my wife loved.

101
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:19,210
She was a human.

102
00:08:20,150 --> 00:08:26,740
I'm merely keeping the promise
I made a long time ago.

103
00:08:29,190 --> 00:08:32,250
But I won't say more on the subject.

104
00:08:33,450 --> 00:08:39,460
I couldn't adequately describe
her face, or voice, or eyes.

105
00:08:39,860 --> 00:08:42,890
This is my memory alone.

106
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:47,200
It's silly, isn't it?

107
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:51,930
I've been keeping a promise
I made to a dead woman.

108
00:08:53,110 --> 00:08:54,610
You're right.

109
00:08:56,530 --> 00:09:03,860
But I'm sure she's glad
you're keeping your promise.

110
00:09:06,110 --> 00:09:10,470
Himmel, you're a good hero.

111
00:09:10,470 --> 00:09:13,520
I'm sure you'll defeat the Demon King.

112
00:09:14,290 --> 00:09:21,770
My wife wished to see the era of peace that
comes after the defeat of the Demon King.

113
00:09:22,700 --> 00:09:29,030
I'll carry the memory of the great
hero Himmel into the future.

114
00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:34,220
I appreciate it, but you
don't have to do that.

115
00:09:35,070 --> 00:09:38,440
I have Frieren and Eisen in my party.

116
00:09:38,910 --> 00:09:41,540
I won't live as long as an elf.

117
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:44,540
Then it'll be Frieren's job.

118
00:09:45,630 --> 00:09:49,320
She'll carry our memory into the future.

119
00:09:50,100 --> 00:09:51,050
Right?

120
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:53,290
Fine, I guess.

121
00:09:54,710 --> 00:09:55,900
I see.

122
00:09:57,520 --> 00:09:59,940
You've been blessed with good companions.

123
00:10:01,510 --> 00:10:03,430
Later, I'll have to show you

124
00:10:03,660 --> 00:10:07,730
the collection of handsome poses
I came up with.

125
00:10:07,730 --> 00:10:10,200
Actually, maybe I won't remember you.

126
00:10:13,140 --> 00:10:17,400
I probably didn't need that collection
of handsome poses after all.

127
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:19,730
I wasted my memory.

128
00:10:20,550 --> 00:10:23,830
Do you remember that hero's face?

129
00:10:24,990 --> 00:10:26,360
Of course I do.

130
00:10:26,740 --> 00:10:27,930
And his voice?

131
00:10:30,110 --> 00:10:32,250
Don't insult me.

132
00:10:32,250 --> 00:10:34,040
I remember everything.

133
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:38,000
Himmel is the reason I decided
I'd get to know people.

134
00:10:38,620 --> 00:10:42,140
Like your reason for defending this village,

135
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:43,930
it's important to me.

136
00:10:45,250 --> 00:10:48,450
I see. A reason, huh?

137
00:10:50,330 --> 00:10:53,490
I can't remember anymore.

138
00:10:54,120 --> 00:10:58,990
Not her face, nor her voice, nor her eyes.

139
00:11:00,780 --> 00:11:07,360
But I continue defending this village
for an important something.

140
00:11:10,510 --> 00:11:13,320
You're quite the jokester, Old Man Voll.

141
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:20,850
By the way, where are you headed?

142
00:11:20,850 --> 00:11:24,860
We're heading to Aureole,
the land where souls rest.

143
00:11:25,430 --> 00:11:27,050
Where is that?

144
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:30,510
Ende, where the Demon King's castle is.

145
00:11:31,030 --> 00:11:32,300
I see.

146
00:11:34,660 --> 00:11:38,090
So you're finally off
to defeat the Demon King.

147
00:11:40,790 --> 00:11:44,780
I hope that brings peace to the land.

148
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:53,210
Old Man Voll, the Demon King is...

149
00:12:03,790 --> 00:12:05,110
What is it?

150
00:12:06,430 --> 00:12:11,460
I'll carry your memory into the future, too.

151
00:12:15,390 --> 00:12:18,280
I wouldn't mind that.

152
00:12:22,220 --> 00:12:26,670
I'm glad I got to see you
at the end of my life.

153
00:12:26,670 --> 00:12:30,100
You said the same thing eighty years ago.

154
00:12:39,020 --> 00:12:42,380
Goodbye, Old Man Voll. Take care.

155
00:12:44,040 --> 00:12:45,260
Frieren.

156
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:49,550
I dreamed of my wife.

157
00:12:50,370 --> 00:12:51,280
I see.

158
00:12:52,570 --> 00:12:57,400
Maybe it's because you and I
reminisced about the past.

159
00:12:59,360 --> 00:13:00,490
See you later.

160
00:13:23,030 --> 00:13:24,320
Were you asking him something?

161
00:13:24,780 --> 00:13:26,560
I'm looking for someone.

162
00:13:28,530 --> 00:13:31,810
You remember why I'm on
this journey, don't you?

163
00:13:32,370 --> 00:13:35,680
Isn't it to travel with a sexy older woman?

164
00:13:36,170 --> 00:13:38,690
That's important, too.

165
00:13:40,130 --> 00:13:42,690
But this is the goal of my journey.

166
00:13:43,510 --> 00:13:47,320
To reunite with my friend
who departed ten years ago.

167
00:13:47,320 --> 00:13:49,340
Oh, right.

168
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:53,590
A photo? You don't see those often.

169
00:13:54,380 --> 00:13:58,160
It was taken by a mage who visited
my village a long time ago.

170
00:13:59,250 --> 00:14:01,540
Have you figured out where he went?

171
00:14:01,540 --> 00:14:06,020
We've been traveling along major
roads in the northern lands.

172
00:14:06,530 --> 00:14:08,960
Along the way, we've met
people who have seen him.

173
00:14:09,410 --> 00:14:12,190
We can probably keep heading north.

174
00:14:15,660 --> 00:14:17,220
That was ten years ago, right?

175
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:19,910
I'm surprised anyone remembers seeing him.

176
00:14:19,910 --> 00:14:21,970
He has a unique name.

177
00:14:22,370 --> 00:14:24,010
It leaves a strong impression.

178
00:14:24,710 --> 00:14:26,230
What's his name?

179
00:14:26,720 --> 00:14:28,460
Gorilla Warrior.

180
00:14:32,260 --> 00:14:33,230
Yeah, okay.

181
00:14:34,830 --> 00:14:35,870
So what's his real name?

182
00:14:38,320 --> 00:14:39,360
His real name?

183
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:41,330
Are you sure you guys were best friends?

184
00:14:41,620 --> 00:14:45,950
Everyone in the village always
called him Gorilla Warrior.

185
00:14:45,950 --> 00:14:48,750
Actually, he made them call him that.

186
00:14:48,750 --> 00:14:50,630
How does that even happen?

187
00:14:52,380 --> 00:14:54,850
By the way, I'm Goatee Priest.

188
00:14:54,850 --> 00:14:56,130
No one asked.

189
00:14:56,500 --> 00:14:59,790
Did people call you that even
before you could grow a goatee?

190
00:14:59,790 --> 00:15:01,840
Are we still talking about this?

191
00:15:02,390 --> 00:15:06,790
Either way, it's good enough if they
recognize the name Gorilla Warrior.

192
00:15:06,790 --> 00:15:09,410
He'd definitely be calling himself that.

193
00:15:10,050 --> 00:15:11,410
Sounds like a weird guy.

194
00:15:11,670 --> 00:15:14,790
Yeah. He's a weird guy.

195
00:15:22,860 --> 00:15:26,110
Once we cross this canyon
and walk for about a week,

196
00:15:26,110 --> 00:15:28,930
we'll finally be in the
magical city of Äußerst.

197
00:15:30,600 --> 00:15:33,170
This really has been a long journey.

198
00:15:33,170 --> 00:15:34,900
What are you looking at me for?

199
00:15:35,420 --> 00:15:38,530
I didn't do anything wrong this time.

200
00:15:39,190 --> 00:15:41,720
Can we stop by a village first?

201
00:15:42,030 --> 00:15:45,060
The road branches here.

202
00:15:45,060 --> 00:15:48,230
Sure. We'd like to rest, too.

203
00:15:52,910 --> 00:15:57,240
Ten years ago, did someone who called
himself "Gorilla" come this way?

204
00:15:57,640 --> 00:15:59,510
That's a wild question.

205
00:15:59,510 --> 00:16:02,170
Ah, you must mean Mr. Gorilla Warrior.

206
00:16:02,170 --> 00:16:03,710
Wow, he knows who that is.

207
00:16:03,710 --> 00:16:07,080
I remember him, since he
left a strong impression.

208
00:16:07,080 --> 00:16:10,320
He defeated a monster that
appeared near the village.

209
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:13,650
Do you know which way he headed after that?

210
00:16:13,650 --> 00:16:15,600
I'm not sure...

211
00:16:16,590 --> 00:16:17,310
Oh!

212
00:16:17,620 --> 00:16:22,370
Mr. Gorilla seemed close with the stubborn
old woman who lives on the plateau.

213
00:16:22,370 --> 00:16:24,040
You should ask her.

214
00:16:24,040 --> 00:16:27,670
"Stubborn old woman"?
That's quite the nickname.

215
00:16:28,690 --> 00:16:31,710
I'm a stubborn old woman, you see.

216
00:16:31,920 --> 00:16:34,380
I'm not telling you for free.

217
00:16:34,380 --> 00:16:36,870
She even calls herself stubborn.

218
00:16:36,870 --> 00:16:38,450
What should we do?

219
00:16:42,850 --> 00:16:46,470
You'll perform several tasks for me.

220
00:16:46,950 --> 00:16:52,890
First, deliver this letter to Nagel,
a blacksmith in the neighboring town.

221
00:16:55,460 --> 00:16:57,350
It's just a simple errand.

222
00:16:57,650 --> 00:17:00,100
It reminds me of my journey with Himmel.

223
00:17:00,580 --> 00:17:02,850
You did things like this?

224
00:17:03,650 --> 00:17:07,370
Helping people by running errands
was a daily occurrence.

225
00:17:07,870 --> 00:17:13,770
And it always turned into troublesome
searches or monster hunts.

226
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:16,540
Don't jinx us.

227
00:17:17,260 --> 00:17:21,240
In any case, let's do what we can
to get her to open up to us.

228
00:17:47,670 --> 00:17:51,060
This old woman just refuses to open up.

229
00:17:51,060 --> 00:17:53,900
They don't call her stubborn for nothing.

230
00:17:54,210 --> 00:18:00,020
Sorry. Himmel or Heiter could've
gotten her to open up in half a day.

231
00:18:00,020 --> 00:18:03,610
But our party only has
socially awkward members.

232
00:18:07,540 --> 00:18:09,020
This is the last one.

233
00:18:09,020 --> 00:18:12,540
Polish the heroes' statue in the canyon.

234
00:18:14,130 --> 00:18:16,900
Come with me. I'll show you the way.

235
00:18:17,930 --> 00:18:19,880
What heroes' statue?

236
00:18:19,880 --> 00:18:25,440
It's a stone statue of the heroes said
to have saved the world a long time ago.

237
00:18:25,710 --> 00:18:28,050
That's all I know.

238
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:33,030
They're forgotten heroes.
No one even knows their names.

239
00:18:33,030 --> 00:18:35,620
The village has been looking
after it for generations.

240
00:18:36,020 --> 00:18:38,610
Forgotten heroes, huh?

241
00:18:38,610 --> 00:18:41,820
By the way, you're Goatee Priest,
aren't you?

242
00:18:43,460 --> 00:18:44,960
How'd you know?

243
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:50,720
We're here.

244
00:18:52,610 --> 00:18:54,700
A priest and a warrior.

245
00:18:55,340 --> 00:18:57,250
It's pretty old.

246
00:18:57,690 --> 00:19:01,290
They kind of look like Mr. Sein
and Mr. Gorilla.

247
00:19:01,290 --> 00:19:05,070
I think I recognize the warrior.

248
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:08,220
It's Kraft.

249
00:19:08,980 --> 00:19:10,820
You're right.

250
00:19:10,820 --> 00:19:11,980
It looks just like him.

251
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:17,570
Shall we get started?

252
00:19:23,040 --> 00:19:24,170
Sein?

253
00:19:24,170 --> 00:19:25,500
I see.

254
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,810
So this person's name is Kraft.

255
00:19:29,350 --> 00:19:30,440
What about this one?

256
00:19:30,440 --> 00:19:32,490
I don't know.

257
00:19:33,510 --> 00:19:34,820
I see.

258
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:40,620
What's this statue?

259
00:19:40,620 --> 00:19:42,610
Don't they look just like us?

260
00:19:43,730 --> 00:19:47,550
According to the chief,
they're ancient heroes.

261
00:19:47,550 --> 00:19:50,020
But nobody even remembers their names.

262
00:19:50,510 --> 00:19:52,840
We won't end up like that.

263
00:19:52,840 --> 00:19:55,010
I refuse to be forgotten.

264
00:19:56,310 --> 00:20:00,330
I'm going to become
an unforgettable hero like Himmel.

265
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:06,830
Even the greatest heroes
are forgotten someday.

266
00:20:07,310 --> 00:20:12,030
Even Himmel, who defeated the
Demon King, will be no exception.

267
00:20:13,770 --> 00:20:17,860
I guess you're right. It's impossible.

268
00:20:21,510 --> 00:20:24,270
You think I could get this big
if I worked out hard enough?

269
00:20:26,330 --> 00:20:28,060
Gorilla Warrior.

270
00:20:28,430 --> 00:20:30,650
From now on, that's my name.

271
00:20:33,290 --> 00:20:34,810
Why?

272
00:20:35,110 --> 00:20:39,930
If I want to become an unforgettable hero,
then I need a memorable name.

273
00:20:43,380 --> 00:20:45,590
You're right.

274
00:20:46,530 --> 00:20:49,730
Then your name is Goatee Priest.

275
00:20:53,910 --> 00:20:55,620
That's a great name.

276
00:20:56,750 --> 00:20:59,620
I'm sure a goatee would look good on you.

277
00:21:05,260 --> 00:21:07,060
We're finally done.

278
00:21:07,350 --> 00:21:09,150
That was a lot of work.

279
00:21:09,150 --> 00:21:12,630
We could've used magic if it
were a bronze statue.

280
00:21:12,630 --> 00:21:16,060
I asked Gorilla to polish it, too,

281
00:21:16,060 --> 00:21:19,470
but he didn't do as good a job.

282
00:21:20,060 --> 00:21:22,690
His only strength is fighting.

283
00:21:23,020 --> 00:21:26,960
But his name sure was memorable.

284
00:21:29,080 --> 00:21:32,950
Gorilla talked about you a lot.

285
00:21:34,260 --> 00:21:40,150
He said the two of you would become heroes
whose names would be remembered forever.

286
00:22:00,460 --> 00:22:02,570
Did you figure out where Gorilla went?

287
00:22:04,400 --> 00:22:05,490
Tür.

288
00:22:06,140 --> 00:22:09,440
It's a trading city in the middle
of the northern lands.

289
00:22:09,440 --> 00:22:11,790
That's a long ways east from here.

290
00:22:12,200 --> 00:22:15,030
It's in the opposite
direction from Äußerst.

291
00:22:15,980 --> 00:22:17,080
Yeah.

292
00:22:21,730 --> 00:22:23,910
What am I going to do?

293
00:22:26,050 --> 00:22:28,590
<i>And you alright</i>

294
00:22:29,090 --> 00:22:31,100
<i>Can you hear me</i>

295
00:22:31,890 --> 00:22:37,980
<i>Trailing along deserted train tracks</i>

296
00:22:37,980 --> 00:22:40,310
<i>Crying excessively</i>

297
00:22:40,900 --> 00:22:43,480
<i>Wanting you to smile</i>

298
00:22:43,480 --> 00:22:49,530
<i>I hold on to memories I'd like to keep fresh</i>

299
00:22:49,530 --> 00:22:53,240
<i>There's something I'd like to tell you</i>

300
00:22:53,240 --> 00:22:56,000
<i>With words more precious than a goodbye</i>

301
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:01,670
<i>Unremarkable yet special</i>

302
00:23:01,670 --> 00:23:05,210
<i>See, things that couldn't have been</i>

303
00:23:05,210 --> 00:23:08,260
<i>Seen if it weren't for these eyes</i>

304
00:23:08,260 --> 00:23:13,010
<i>Why? They begin to overflow</i>

305
00:23:13,010 --> 00:23:19,100
<i>So, if I were to be born again</i>

306
00:23:19,100 --> 00:23:24,940
<i>I would surely choose this place once more</i>

307
00:23:24,940 --> 00:23:30,950
<i>So, if we were to meet again</i>

308
00:23:30,950 --> 00:23:36,700
<i>I'd never let you go, I'd choose the present</i>

309
00:23:36,700 --> 00:23:39,660
<i>Even if no promises were made</i>

310
00:23:39,660 --> 00:23:42,360
<i>Even if I'm lost in days of loneliness</i>

311
00:23:42,360 --> 00:23:48,710
<i>Those tears will be alright,
dawn will surely break</i>

312
00:23:48,710 --> 00:23:51,390
<i>I'm whispering our lullaby</i>

313
00:23:51,390 --> 00:23:55,400
<i>for you to come back home</i>

314
00:23:56,220 --> 00:23:58,890
Join me in the other room, one at a time.

315
00:23:56,650 --> 00:23:59,470
Make yourselves at home.

316
00:23:57,160 --> 00:23:58,890
Yeah, it's all my fault.

317
00:23:58,890 --> 00:24:01,810
Because mediating is a priest's job.

318
00:23:59,490 --> 00:24:01,820
The medicine is ready.

319
00:24:00,690 --> 00:24:01,820
You're embarrassing me.

320
00:24:01,820 --> 00:24:04,260
I'm pretty handsome when
I'm being considerate, aren't I?

321
00:24:02,280 --> 00:24:04,590
I guess it's not so bad.

322
00:24:04,590 --> 00:24:05,930
Take care, then.
